    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '226. A patient with chronic kidney disease has K+ of 6.8 mEq/L. ECG shows peaked T waves but normal QRS width. What is the priority treatment?', '["Emergent dialysis", "IV calcium gluconate for membrane stabilization, then insulin/dextrose for K+ shifting", "Oral kayexalate only", "IV furosemide alone"]', 1, 'Even without QRS widening, peaked T waves with K+ >6.5 warrant IV calcium for cardiac protection. Insulin/dextrose shifts K+ intracellularly within 15-30 min. Dialysis is definitive but takes time to arrange.'),
    (tid, '227. Hypocalcemia on ECG characteristically causes which interval change?', '["Shortened QT", "Prolonged QT interval (specifically the ST segment)", "Shortened PR", "Widened QRS"]', 1, 'Hypocalcemia prolongs the ST segment (not the T wave), lengthening the QT interval. Severe hypocalcemia can cause QT prolongation leading to Torsades de Pointes. IV calcium gluconate is the treatment.'),
    (tid, '228. Hypercalcemia on ECG characteristically causes which change?', '["Prolonged QT", "Shortened QT interval", "ST elevation", "Peaked T waves"]', 1, 'Hypercalcemia shortens the ST segment and QT interval. Severe hypercalcemia (>14 mg/dL) can cause Osborn waves and cardiac arrest. Treatment: IV NS, loop diuretics, calcitonin, bisphosphonates.'),
    (tid, '229. A patient takes an intentional overdose of atenolol. He is bradycardic (HR 35), hypotensive (BP 60/30), and has altered mental status. Atropine and glucagon are ineffective. What is the next treatment?', '["IV calcium", "High-dose insulin-euglycemia therapy (HIE) with glucose support", "IV lipid emulsion only", "Activated charcoal"]', 1, 'HIE (insulin 1 U/kg bolus then 0.5-1 U/kg/hr with D10W-D25W) is emerging as a key therapy for refractory beta-blocker toxicity. It enhances myocardial glucose utilization and improves inotropy.'),
    (tid, '230. A patient presents with anticholinergic toxidrome: tachycardia, mydriasis, dry skin, urinary retention, and agitation. Which cardiac complication is most concerning?', '["Bradycardia", "Wide-complex tachycardia from sodium channel blockade (if TCA ingestion)", "Heart block", "Pericarditis"]', 1, 'While anticholinergic effects cause sinus tachycardia, TCA overdose also blocks fast sodium channels causing QRS widening and potentially fatal wide-complex arrhythmias. The anticholinergic toxidrome may mask the more dangerous sodium channel effects.'),
    (tid, '231. An organophosphate poisoning patient has severe bradycardia and copious secretions (SLUDGE). What is the cardiac-specific treatment?', '["Pralidoxime only", "Atropine titrated to heart rate and drying of secretions", "Epinephrine infusion", "Calcium channel blocker"]', 1, 'Atropine competitively blocks excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, reversing bradycardia and secretions. Large doses may be needed (up to 10-20 mg). Pralidoxime reactivates acetylcholinesterase.'),
    (tid, '232. Methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning can cause cardiac collapse through which mechanism?', '["Direct cardiotoxicity", "Severe metabolic acidosis with high anion gap causing myocardial depression", "Coronary vasospasm", "Volume depletion"]', 1, 'Toxic alcohol metabolites (formic acid from methanol, glycolic/oxalic acid from ethylene glycol) cause severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Profound acidosis (pH <7.0) directly depresses myocardial function.'),
    (tid, '233. A patient with carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning has troponin elevation and ST changes on ECG. Why does CO cause myocardial injury?', '["Direct viral damage", "CO binds hemoglobin (forming carboxyhemoglobin) reducing O2 delivery, AND directly impairs mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase in myocardial cells", "Coronary thrombosis", "Valvular damage"]', 1, 'CO has 250x greater affinity for hemoglobin than O2, reducing O2-carrying capacity. Additionally, CO directly binds myocardial myoglobin and mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing cellular toxicity independent of hypoxia.'),
    (tid, '234. Cyanide poisoning causes cardiac arrest through what mechanism?', '["Coronary vasospasm", "Inhibition of cytochrome oxidase (Complex IV), halting aerobic metabolism and causing cellular asphyxiation", "Sodium channel blockade", "Potassium channel blockade"]', 1, 'Cyanide binds cytochrome oxidase, halting the electron transport chain. Cells cannot utilize oxygen (histotoxic hypoxia), leading to lactic acidosis and cardiovascular collapse despite adequate PaO2.'),
    (tid, '235. What is the antidote kit for cyanide poisoning?', '["N-acetylcysteine", "Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) or sodium thiosulfate + amyl nitrite + sodium nitrite", "Fomepizole", "Deferoxamine"]', 1, 'Hydroxocobalamin (preferred, fewer side effects) directly binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12). The classic three-part kit uses amyl/sodium nitrite (induces methemoglobinemia to bind cyanide) + sodium thiosulfate (detoxification).'),
    (tid, '236. In obstructive shock from tension pneumothorax, what bedside finding distinguishes it from cardiac tamponade?', '["Both have JVD and hypotension", "Absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation (tension pneumo) versus muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus (tamponade)", "They are clinically identical", "Only imaging can differentiate"]', 1, 'Both cause obstructive shock with JVD and hypotension. Tension pneumothorax has unilateral absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation, and hyperresonance. Tamponade has muffled hearts sounds and pulsus paradoxus.'),
    (tid, '237. A patient in septic shock has high cardiac output and low SVR (warm shock). What is the first-line vasopressor?', '["Phenylephrine", "Norepinephrine", "Dopamine", "Epinephrine"]', 1, 'Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for septic shock (Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021). It provides potent alpha-1 vasoconstriction with moderate beta-1 inotropy, restoring SVR and MAP.'),
    (tid, '238. How is neurogenic shock differentiated from other types of shock?', '["Tachycardia and vasoconstriction", "Bradycardia with hypotension (loss of sympathetic tone causing vasodilation) in a patient with spinal cord injury", "High SVR", "Pulmonary edema"]', 1, 'Neurogenic shock from spinal cord injury (typically above T6) causes loss of sympathetic tone resulting in vasodilation (hypotension) and unopposed vagal tone (bradycardia). Warm, dry extremities are characteristic.'),
    (tid, '239. A patient with anaphylactic shock has cardiovascular collapse. Which medication is first-line for both bronchospasm and hypotension?', '["IV diphenhydramine", "IM epinephrine 0.3-0.5 mg (1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh", "Albuterol nebulizer", "IV methylprednisolone"]', 1, 'IM epinephrine is the ONLY first-line treatment for anaphylaxis. It provides alpha-1 vasoconstriction (reverses hypotension), beta-1 inotropy/chronotropy, and beta-2 bronchodilation. Delay increases mortality.'),
    (tid, '240. What differentiates cardiogenic shock from septic shock on a pulmonary artery catheter?', '["Both have the same hemodynamic profile", "Cardiogenic: high PCWP, low CO, high SVR. Septic: low PCWP, high CO, low SVR", "Cardiogenic: low PCWP. Septic: high PCWP", "They cannot be differentiated invasively"]', 1, 'Cardiogenic = pump failure (high PCWP from congestion, low CO, compensatory high SVR). Septic = distributive (low PCWP from vasodilation, high CO from hyperdynamic state, low SVR from vasodilation).'),
    (tid, '241. A patient with suspected cardiac tamponade has equalization of diastolic pressures on right heart catheterization. What does this confirm?', '["Normal hemodynamics", "Tamponade physiology (RA, RV diastolic, PA diastolic, and PCWP are all nearly equal)", "Severe aortic stenosis", "Constrictive pericarditis only"]', 1, 'Diastolic pressure equalization (RA ≈ RV diastolic ≈ PA diastolic ≈ PCWP, typically 15-25 mmHg) is the hemodynamic hallmark of tamponade. It occurs because external compression equalizes all chamber filling pressures.'),
    (tid, '242. What is the Fick principle used for in cardiac emergencies?', '["Calculating ejection fraction", "Estimating cardiac output based on oxygen consumption and arteriovenous oxygen content difference", "Measuring coronary blood flow", "Determining valve area"]', 1, 'Fick equation: CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2). It estimates cardiac output from oxygen consumption divided by the difference in arterial and venous oxygen content. Used when thermodilution is unreliable.'),
    (tid, '243. A patient on clozapine develops fever, tachycardia, and elevated troponin with new heart failure symptoms. What cardiac complication should be suspected?', '["MI from atherosclerosis", "Drug-induced myocarditis (clozapine-associated)", "Pericarditis", "Endocarditis"]', 1, 'Clozapine is associated with potentially fatal myocarditis, typically within the first 4 weeks of treatment. It causes eosinophilic myocardial inflammation. Requires immediate drug discontinuation and supportive care.'),
    (tid, '244. Anthracycline (doxorubicin) cardiotoxicity is dose-dependent and causes what type of cardiac injury?', '["Acute pericarditis", "Dose-dependent dilated cardiomyopathy from oxidative damage to cardiomyocytes", "Valvular stenosis", "Coronary vasospasm"]', 1, 'Anthracyclines generate free radicals that damage cardiomyocyte mitochondria and DNA. Risk increases with cumulative doses >400-550 mg/m2. Presents as dilated cardiomyopathy months to years after treatment.'),
    (tid, '245. 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) cardiac toxicity most commonly presents as what?', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy", "Coronary vasospasm mimicking ACS with chest pain and ST changes", "Endocarditis", "Arrhythmias only"]', 1, '5-FU causes coronary vasospasm in 1-18% of patients, presenting with anginal chest pain and ECG changes during or shortly after infusion. Can cause MI and death. Treatment: stop 5-FU, give nitrates and CCBs.'),
    (tid, '246. Radiation-induced heart disease (RIHD) affects which cardiac structures?', '["Only the myocardium", "All structures: pericardium (most common), coronary arteries, valves, myocardium, and conduction system", "Only the valves", "Only the coronary arteries"]', 1, 'RIHD affects all cardiac structures. Pericarditis/pericardial fibrosis is most common. Coronary disease (ostial lesions), valvular fibrosis, restrictive cardiomyopathy, and conduction abnormalities can occur years to decades later.'),
    (tid, '247. Thyroid storm can cause cardiac decompensation through what mechanism?', '["Coronary thrombosis", "Excess thyroid hormone increases beta-adrenergic receptor sensitivity, causing tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, and high-output heart failure", "Direct myocardial toxicity", "Valvular destruction"]', 1, 'Thyroid hormone sensitizes the heart to catecholamines and increases metabolic demand. Thyroid storm causes severe tachycardia, AF (10-35%), and high-output heart failure. Beta-blockers are essential treatment.'),
    (tid, '248. What cardiac emergency is associated with severe hypothyroidism (myxedema coma)?', '["Hypertensive crisis", "Bradycardia, pericardial effusion, decreased cardiac output, and risk of cardiac arrest", "Tachyarrhythmias", "Aortic dissection"]', 1, 'Severe hypothyroidism depresses myocardial function causing bradycardia, decreased contractility, pericardial effusion, and prolonged QT. Myxedema coma has 20-50% mortality. IV levothyroxine and hydrocortisone are treatment.'),
    (tid, '249. A patient with Addison disease (adrenal crisis) presents with refractory hypotension and bradycardia despite fluids and vasopressors. What medication is lifesaving?', '["Insulin", "IV hydrocortisone 100mg", "Calcium gluconate", "Glucagon"]', 1, 'Adrenal crisis causes cortisol deficiency leading to vasodilatory shock unresponsive to catecholamines. IV hydrocortisone 100mg restores vascular responsiveness to vasopressors and is lifesaving.'),
    (tid, '250. Fat embolism syndrome (FES) after long bone fracture can cause cardiac complications through what mechanism?', '["Coronary thrombosis", "RV strain from pulmonary vascular obstruction by fat globules, plus inflammatory mediator release causing myocardial depression", "Valvular damage", "Pericardial effusion"]', 1, 'Fat globules embolize to the pulmonary vasculature causing mechanical obstruction and RV strain. Lipase breaks down fat into free fatty acids that trigger inflammatory injury to the pulmonary endothelium and myocardium.');
